      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Acute reduction in dietary energy intake reduces very low-density lipoprotein triglyceride (VLDL-TG) concentration. Although chronic dietary energy surplus and obesity are associated with hypertriglyceridemia, the effect of acute overfeeding on VLDL-TG metabolism is not known."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The aim of the present study was to investigate the effects of acute negative and positive energy balance on VLDL-TG metabolism in healthy women."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Ten healthy women (AGE: 22.0±2.9 years, BMI: 21.2±1.3 kg/m(2)) underwent a stable isotopically labeled tracer infusion study to determine basal VLDL-TG kinetics after performing, in random order, three experimental trials on the previous day: i) isocaloric feeding (control) ii) hypocaloric feeding with a dietary energy restriction of 2.89±0.42 MJ and iii) hypercaloric feeding with a dietary energy surplus of 2.91±0.32 MJ. The three diets had the same macronutrient composition."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Fasting plasma VLDL-TG concentrations decreased by ∼26% after hypocaloric feeding relative to the control trial (P\u200a=\u200a0.037), owing to decreased hepatic VLDL-TG secretion rate (by 21%, P\u200a=\u200a0.023) and increased VLDL-TG plasma clearance rate (by ∼12%, P\u200a=\u200a0.016). Hypercaloric feeding increased plasma glucose concentration (P\u200a=\u200a0.042) but had no effect on VLDL-TG concentration and kinetics compared to the control trial."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Acute dietary energy deficit (∼3MJ) leads to hypotriglyceridemia via a combination of decreased hepatic VLDL-TG secretion and increased VLDL-TG clearance. On the other hand, acute dietary energy surplus (∼3MJ) does not affect basal VLDL-TG metabolism but disrupts glucose homeostasis in healthy women."
        }
      ]},
